artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the scalar delay differential equation
  dx/dt = -D(x(t)) + P · x(t-s) · exp(-x(t-s)),  t ≥ 0,
where x(t) ≥ 0 denotes the size (or biomass) of a population at time t, P > 0 is an egg-production (recruitment) parameter, s > 0 is a constant maturation delay, and D : [0,∞) → [0,∞) is a nonlinear density-dependent mortality function. Two prototype forms are considered:
  (i) D(x) = a x / (x + b), with constants a, b > 0;
  (ii) D(x) = a − b e^{-x}, with constants a, b > 0 (so that D(0) = a − b, and D is increasing and bounded above by a).
For nonnegative initial data φ ∈ C([-s, 0], [0,∞)), give a complete qualitative analysis of the resulting dynamics. In particular, determine:
  (a) existence, uniqueness, positivity and boundedness (permanence) of solutions;
  (b) the existence, multiplicity and local/global stability of nonnegative equilibria, as functions of the parameters (P, s, a, b);
  (c) conditions for global attractivity of a positive equilibrium and conditions for its loss of stability (including Hopf bifurcations as the delay s varies);
  (d) the occurrence of periodic, almost periodic, or more complex (e.g. chaotic) asymptotic behaviour;
  (e) the qualitative effects, relevant to fishery-type models, that distinguish the dynamics produced by such nonlinear mortality terms D(x) from those of the classical linear-mortality Nicholson model D(x) = δ x.

